WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:07.000
Do you often find yourself getting into arguments
with people over stupid stuff, like why the

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.020
Earth cannot possibly be flat or whether the
moon is genuinely made of cheese?

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.090
And, in your own mind, you are almost 100%
certain that the Earth is oblately spherical

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:24.570
and the moon is, unfortunately not made of
delicious melty cheddar but boring old rocks.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:31.380
Yet incredibly you end up losing these arguments
against utterly moronic people.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.030
You know that you should have won the argument
and you provided them with countless irrefutable

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:44.620
evidence yet no matter what you say, you somehow
fail to change their mind, or remotely shake

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.720
their ostentatious self-confidence in their
erroneous views.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.160
Why is it so difficult to argue with stupid
people?

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.470
Why do they refuse to listen to empirical
evidence and instead favour the wisdom that

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:00.489
the smelly old weird bloke told them at the
pub?

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:06.100
And why do they often think themselves more
intelligent than their words and actions would

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.530
lead you to believe?

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.210
Let’s find out.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:14.179
Neuroscientists think there are very real
and fascinating reasons and explanations for

00:01:14.179 --> 00:01:19.079
this frustrating phenomenon but before we
talk about these, we must establish whether

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:25.179
our suspicions are true, do stupid people
think they’re smarter than they are.

00:01:25.179 --> 00:01:31.359
Over the past twenty years, two social psychologists
have been attempting to answer this question,

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:33.889
David Dunning and Justin Kruger.

00:01:33.889 --> 00:01:39.219
David Dunning was inspired to begin this research
after reading a feature in the 1996 World

00:01:39.219 --> 00:01:40.219
Almanac.

00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:47.369
About a Pittsburgh bank robber, McArthur Wheeler,
who held up a bank at gunpoint, in broad daylight,

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:48.819
without a mask.

00:01:48.819 --> 00:01:53.710
But strangely his face was covered in lemon
juice.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:59.180
When the police arrested him later at his
home he said: “but I wore the juice”.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:05.399
He explained how, since lemon juice is sometimes
used as invisible ink he thought that by covering

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:12.670
his face in lemon juice he would become invisible
to other people and the security cameras.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:17.400
He apparently took a picture of himself whilst
wearing the lemon juice before the robbery

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:23.920
to confirm his theory and remarkably, albeit
according to Wheeler’s own account, his

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.460
face was not visible in the photo - police
were never quite able to explain this.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.420
But then, this is a man who tried to make
himself invisible with lemon juice so I wouldn’t

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.370
put too much thought into it.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:42.920
Inspired by this blatant idiocy Dunning teamed
up with Kruger and conducted a series of experiments

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:48.940
in which they got participants to rate themselves
on their grammar, logical reasoning and sense

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.350
of humour.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:55.660
They then compared the participant’s self-appraisals
with the results of a series of tests they

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.230
asked them to complete.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:03.760
The studies all concluded that people consistently
rate themselves as being far more competent

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.340
at everything than they actually are.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:12.140
Most people in the studies performed far worse
on the tests than they believed they had done.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:18.040
A poll in 1980 conducted by a Swedish Psychologist
illustrated this nicely when it showed that

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:26.720
93% of American drivers considered their driving
ability to be above average - just think about

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.850
that for a second, the maths don’t quite
add up.

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:36.220
The research shows that an overestimation
of our talents is something we, mostly, all

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:42.650
do, albeit some of us significantly more so
than others and that the more inferior a person’s

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:48.360
talents and/or intelligence the greater the
degree with which they overestimate these.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:54.630
One of the biggest factors driving this is
the brain’s natural egocentric bias.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:04:01.650
First identified by psychologists in 1979,
it turns out that the brain absolutely loves

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:07.060
itself and does everything it can to make
itself look good.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:12.180
Repeated studies have demonstrated the egocentric
bias is present in all types and ages.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:18.280
For example, we tend to associate ourselves
closely with positive events, and we take

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:25.080
credit for things that other people achieve,
such as “My team played well” whereas

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:31.030
when our favourite sports team loses we try
to distance ourselves “They performed poorly”.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:36.509
Similarly, we have no problem when we are
overpaid for work but we think it’s unfair

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:37.699
when others are.

00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:42.360
We think that anyone who drives faster than
us is a lunatic yet anyone who drives slower

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.499
is an idiot.

00:04:44.499 --> 00:04:50.029
You get the idea, your brain thinks it is
the best thing since single-celled organisms.

00:04:50.029 --> 00:04:54.349
But how does this relate to winning arguments?

00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:59.120
Basically, the brain will do anything it needs
to in order to save face.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.150
Your brain must look good no matter what - even
if this involves ignoring facts, making up

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:09.259
facts or browbeating your intellectually-superior
opponent into submission.

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:15.279
Your brain isn’t being a dick for the sake
of it - evolutionarily the egocentric bias

00:05:15.279 --> 00:05:19.059
makes a lot of sense for self-preservation.

00:05:19.059 --> 00:05:24.610
The hunter that steals the prey from other
hunters is more likely to survive.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:30.860
Our egocentric bias is so strong that even
when we irrevocably lose an argument, we convince

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.610
ourselves in our own minds that we somehow
won.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:39.400
As the legendary author Dale Carnegie of the
seminal “How to Win Friends And Influence

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:45.300
People” wrote, “You can’t win an argument
- Nine times out of ten, an argument ends

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:52.309
with each of the contestants more firmly convinced
than ever that he is absolutely right.”

00:05:52.309 --> 00:05:58.110
There is, however, another reason why learned
people often walk away from arguments with

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:04.779
those who possess the intellectual aptitude
of a veritable sea sponge feeling wholly disappointed.

00:06:04.779 --> 00:06:12.309
There is a general fear amongst the human
race of intelligence, even amongst the intelligentsia

00:06:12.309 --> 00:06:13.309
themselves.

00:06:13.309 --> 00:06:19.599
This is a very real phenomenon that numerous
neuroscientists have observed - true, we admire

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:24.930
those of heightened intelligence yet we simultaneously
fear them.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:29.930
In modern society, we have no need to fear
those of superior physical might over oneself,

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.660
unless you just made a pass at their wife.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:40.139
But say you meet a huge burly bloke at a dinner
party with guns larger than your entire torso.

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:44.479
Most of us could converse with him rather
happily, without feeling intimidated.

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:51.339
Whereas if we meet a weedy professor of astrophysics
at a dinner party that knows general relativity

00:06:51.339 --> 00:06:56.509
better than you know where you left your car
keys, then suddenly, and for reasons which

00:06:56.509 --> 00:07:01.490
are unclear to yourself, you feel intimidated
by him.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:08.009
Perhaps you don’t, if so, congratulations,
you’re probably rather intelligent or a

00:07:08.009 --> 00:07:09.009
psychopath.

00:07:09.009 --> 00:07:14.029
The reason for this disparity is strangely
related to the egocentric brain.

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:21.009
We know how the bodybuilder gained his mass,
by relentlessly pounding the gym and protein.

00:07:21.009 --> 00:07:26.620
We know that if we could be half as arsed
or inclined as he then we too could match

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:29.569
his level of esteemed physical prowess.

00:07:29.569 --> 00:07:36.539
And most importantly, we can usually predict
his intentions and actions, we don’t need

00:07:36.539 --> 00:07:41.219
to be as strong as him to know what he might
do - if we piss him off, he hits us in the

00:07:41.219 --> 00:07:42.479
face.

00:07:42.479 --> 00:07:48.139
Yet the scientist, unless we are intellectually
superior than he, and even if we are - then

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:52.559
we will be unable to predict what he might
say or do.

00:07:52.559 --> 00:07:57.839
He could say something that will reveal our
lack of intelligence and make us look stupid.

00:07:57.839 --> 00:08:04.969
Thus we fear him, to compound this fear, unlike
the bodybuilder most of us will feel that

00:08:04.969 --> 00:08:10.150
even if we read 100 books we could never reach
the scientist’s level of intelligence.

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:17.430
And rather pessimistically, research supports
the idea that many people are most likely,

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.440
unable to.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:24.360
As I have previously covered in another video
- we all have the ability to improve our crystalised

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:32.570
intelligence, our bank of facts and knowledge,
but once we hit our mid-20s the brain becomes

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:42.110
less plastic, it is not totally, but mostly
set in its ways.

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:47.880
Like a car that has already been built, parts
can be swapped out and polished up but ultimately

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:52.620
there’s a limit to what one can do with
its performance.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:58.460
And thus, after this age, we lose the ability
to improve our fluid intelligence - that is

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:04.710
the ability to pick up new skills quickly,
think on the spot, outwit your opponents and

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.210
problem solve.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:12.470
So if the scientist has, not only impressive
crystalised intelligence but also greater

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:19.370
fluid intelligence than us, then we subconsciously
know that this is a state of mental acuity

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:24.200
that we are most likely, unable to ever reach.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:29.960
Biologically we are hard-wired to be scared
of this prospect and we will feel a natural

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:34.050
propensity to repel that which we cannot better.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:41.290
So you may defensively say something stupid
to the scientist, even jokingly, such as ‘oh,

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:46.720
a scientist, you think you’re so clever,
don’t you?’ even if he gave us no obvious

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.860
reason to feel so intimidated.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:56.430
This has a reciprocal effect - clever people
are generally well aware of others fear and

00:09:56.430 --> 00:10:02.180
distrust of their intelligence and so feel
less self-confident when in argument or debate

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:06.090
with a peer, even if they know they could
easily win.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:12.900
Also, most people become intelligence because
they have a proclivity to question their surroundings

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.960
and their self.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:20.110
Intelligent people are generally more aware
of the fact that they don’t know everything

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:25.410
- especially in the scientific fields it is
normal and expected for one to criticise their

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:30.510
own theories and for colleagues and peers
to cross-examine them.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:35.390
Thus smart people will always question whether
they are as intelligent as they think they

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:40.901
are, whether they put their own point across
adequately, and if, perhaps their opponent

00:10:40.901 --> 00:10:47.930
does, in fact, have a point in their ardent
cheesy moon and flat Earth beliefs, as bonkers

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:50.240
as they may sound.

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:56.200
So they will come away from the debate with
their head swirling with questions and self-doubt.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:11:03.280
Conversely, stupid people are far less likely
to question things including themselves, they

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:09.060
tend to be less introspective, that’s probably
why they are still so stupid.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:15.700
So they seem and are far more confident in
their moronic, unfounded theories.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:20.720
They don’t need to question them or see
how they stack up when confronted with empirical

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.450
evidence because they have never done so before
and they possess not a shred of self-doubt

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:27.450
anyway.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:32.770
To add to all this, as the Dunning-Kruger
effect proved, stupid people rarely think

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:35.470
of themselves as stupid.

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:42.090
They have absolutely no idea that they have
less intelligence than a Swiss cheese.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:48.360
As Dunning and Kruger’s research demonstrated,
there is a direct correlation between a lack

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:54.190
of intelligence and one’s high confidence
in their intelligence and vice-versa.

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:59.620
To put it simply, inept people think they
are proficient and proficient people think

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:02.170
themselves less so.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:07.820
Keep in mind that the Dunning Kruger effect
is not an absolute but a general trend, not

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:13.080
all stupid people overestimate their intelligence
and some intelligent people are very aware

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.670
of how smart they are and will happily tell
anyone who will listen.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:22.170
I’ll leave you with a great quote by the
late great comedian George Carlin that anyone

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:28.540
who has ever despaired with human stupidity
will sympathise with “Think of how stupid

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:33.970
the average person is, and realize half of
them are stupider than that.”

